when I was six years old my family went  camping on the beach in the effort to  teach us kids what it meant to be Native  Hawaiian we would gather in tide pools  we cut fish we learned how to cook them  and played games and in the evening our  uncle a very loving and kind man named  mr. ko Noah would tell us stories about  our ancestors so one night he gathered  us together in a circle and he told us  about these great navigators who sailed  on these little canoes across the  world's largest ocean using the Stars  and the Sun and the moon to guide them  to find tiny little islands scattered  across this great expanse the stories  were awesome and we were hooked but mr.  Kona could see in our eyes that he could  have been talking about the Trojans or  the Vikings because to us there's really  no difference those people that he was  talking about in those stories seemed  kind of distant sort of abstract to  belong to us  to me so he asked us everybody stand up  so we did he said each one of you are  descended from somebody like that in one  of those stories and lined up behind  each one of us are members of our entire  family who came before that if we ever  fall they'll be right there to catch you  and if you're lucky really lucky and you  walk slowly ever so slowly and then  quickly look back you just might see one  of them out of the corner of your eye  try it okay so we started walking kind  of awkwardly slowly ever so slowly we  quickly turn back in anticipation did  you see them yeah well what did they  look like Oh mine was seven feet tall  with big muscles  said one of the show-off boys my best  friend she said hers had beautiful skin  with long black hair I didn't say  anything but I was sure that I saw my  great-great-grandmother in a tenth of a  dress her silver hair pulled back  looking at me with a stern but approving  look looking into her eyes it was like  looking into my own in the mirror at  that moment for the rest of my life  I knew that my ancestors were real that  everywhere I went no matter how much  trouble I was in no matter how lonely I  felt they would be right there with me  to help but why am I talking about  ancestors because we all have them and  whether we're aware of it or not they  have the power to affect profoundly our  lives our ancestors we are of them and  they are in us most of us recognize that  right in our physical traits kind of  family resemblances like I have my  mother's eyes or maybe in mannerisms I'm  not sure where I get that from but  scientists are discovering through a new  field called behavioral epigenetics that  not only do we get these physical traits  passed down but the experiences and  actions of our ancestors get passed to  us as memory through tiny little  switches in our DNA so in other words  our experiences they get stored in our  genes they become part of us and then we  pass them down to our offspring so for  example victims of trauma you know  poverty war childhood abuse they carry  around that trauma not just as memories  but in molecular scars so that handsome  chin of yours that chiseled went from  your grandfather may not be the only  thing you inherited from him you may  have also inherited his predisposition  to depression based on something he  suffered but the good news is not only  do kind of our deficits and our  mrs. get passed down but so do our  strengths and our resilience is we  inherit loving behavior we inherit  kindness we inherit positive outlooks so  that eternal optimism that you have that  ability to overcome to persevere likely  came from somebody in your family line  who overcame something and then passed  those awesome traits unto you so I  started thinking what kind of ancestor  do I want to be what kind of negative  things am i carrying around some traits  that or things that I'm creating that I  don't want to pass on conversely what  are those positive things those awesome  traits or those things I have yet to  create that I want to make sure that I  do some of those people those survivors  they did that they made choices to  profoundly change the future of their  families by kind of overriding those  junk and creating new memories to create  a new path and we can do that too  but it gets better much more interesting  so now we'll need to be pass all this  stuff down through our DNA but our  ancestors can actually also help us  perform better a study recently by the  European Journal of Social Psychology  showed that simply thinking about our  genetic origin helps us perform better  so I thought about that and I thought in  these studies you know these people did  puzzles or they ran obstacle courses and  just being triggered to think about  their ancestor they did better and you  know that's happened to me so earlier in  my career I was a diver I used to go in  these small little inflatable boats to  remote islands in the Pacific and study  coral reefs and on my very first trip I  was terrified 1,200 miles away from  safety and I knew that in every place we  were gonna explore we were probably  gonna encounter sharks but I came from  ocean people remember those guys and  those tiny canoes that sailed across  that huge ocean yeah when I thought  about that and I remembered that my fear  went away I don't mean to sound  dramatics I know that kind of does but  really it was incredible even to me how  I could do things  that I had never done before simply by  drawing on their courage and their  ancient wisdom of the sea thinking about  them gave me the confidence to perform  better and then kind of drawing from  their memory and their experience I  figured out how it's pretty it's pretty  powerful so we all have relatively  complex brains right so imagine what  ancestral wisdom like that is stored up  in you from the thousands of years since  your ancestors were born talk about Big  Data imagine if we could tap into that  ancestral databank to help us make  better choices now to help us perform  better we only get one chance at this  thing called life right so all of those  choices and the way we perform that  tells us what we're going to leave for  the future so now that we know that even  if we don't know who those ancestors are  we carry their wisdom and their strength  with us  what kind of ancestors do we together  wanna be to leave society better so we  start off as descendants of our  ancestors in life but at some point  either directly or indirectly we end up  as ancestors of our descendants our  choices our decisions are going to  affect what happens in future  generations this idea became kind of  came home to me on the birth of my first  son coming all when he was born my  journey to become an ancestor had begun  it had become my legacy to think about  his so when I think about looking back  now I don't just look back for me I have  to look back and ask how to help me to  help him a couple weeks ago my middle  son eye Lama was playing on a courtyard  and the courtyard right here in campus  and he was kind of looking back and  talking to someone so I thought he was  playing ninjas or pirates one of the  imaginary places he loves to go and I  said hey who are you this time he said  no one mama as he looked at his shoes  I was just having a hard day so I've  turned around to see who was there help  I don't even remember telling my son  that story in fact I'm not even sure I  ever did but I can't tell you how much  peace that gives me as a mother to know  that even when I'm not there to protect  my son  to give him guidance that he knows his  ancestors are real and that if he ever  needs them he just has to turn around  ancestors we all have them I encourage  you to tap into your ancestral bank say  honor those who came before to think  about how to do awesome now and to leave  a super great future for those to follow  ask yourself what kind of ancestor do  you want to be thank you very much
 

